@startuml admin-registration
title Registration - the six steps of the handout scenario

autonumber
skinparam shadowing false

participant "AuthController" as Ctl
participant "RegistrationService" as Svc
participant "PersonsApi\n(generated client)" as Person
participant "KeycloakAdminGateway" as Admin
participant "KeycloakOidcGateway" as Oidc
participant "person-service" as Ps
participant "Keycloak" as Kc

Ctl -> Svc : register(RegistrationRequest)
activate Svc

== 1. the domain user is created first ==

Svc -> Person : registerPerson(email, firstName, lastName)
activate Person
Person -> Ps : POST /api/v1/persons/registration\n{ "email", "firstName", "lastName" }
Ps --> Person : 201 { "userUid": "6f1d2a4e-..." }
Person --> Svc : userUid
deactivate Person

note right of Svc
  The password never travels here.
  person-service owns the profile,
  Keycloak owns the credentials.

  This step comes first on purpose:
  if it fails, no Keycloak account
  was created, so nothing is left
  half-made.
end note

== 2. this service authenticates as itself ==

Svc -> Admin : createUser(email, firstName, lastName, userUid)
activate Admin

Admin -> Oidc : serviceAccountToken()
activate Oidc
Oidc -> Kc : POST /realms/{realm}/protocol/openid-connect/token\nform: grant_type=client_credentials\n      client_id, client_secret
Kc --> Oidc : 200 { access_token, ... }
Oidc --> Admin : admin access token
deactivate Oidc

== 3. the account, without a password yet ==

Admin -> Kc : POST /admin/realms/{realm}/users\nAuthorization: Bearer <admin token>
activate Kc
note right of Kc
  {
    "username": "user@dezxxx.com",
    "email":    "user@dezxxx.com",
    "firstName": "Ivan",
    "lastName":  "Ivanov",
    "enabled": true,
    "emailVerified": false,
    "attributes": { "user_uid": ["6f1d2a4e-..."] }
  }

  No credentials here - the handout
  sets the password separately.
  user_uid rides along as a custom
  attribute (step 8 of the scenario).
end note
Kc --> Admin : 201 Created\nLocation: .../users/0b7a5f2c-19d4-...\n(no body at all)
deactivate Kc

note right of Admin
  The new id arrives only in the
  Location header. Parsing it out
  is this gateway's job.
end note

Admin --> Svc : keycloakUserId
deactivate Admin

== 4. the password ==

Svc -> Admin : setPassword(keycloakUserId, password)
activate Admin
Admin -> Oidc : serviceAccountToken()
Oidc --> Admin : admin access token
Admin -> Kc : PUT /admin/realms/{realm}/users/{id}/reset-password\n{ "type": "password",\n  "value": "Str0ngP@ssw0rd",\n  "temporary": false }
Kc --> Admin : 204 No Content
Admin --> Svc : done
deactivate Admin

note over Admin, Kc
  temporary MUST be false. Left at Keycloak's
  default the account is created successfully
  and the very next login fails with
  400 (Bad Request) / invalid_grant
  "Account is not fully set up".
end note

== 5. an ordinary login, nothing special ==

Svc -> Oidc : login(email, password)
activate Oidc
Oidc -> Kc : POST /realms/{realm}/protocol/openid-connect/token\nform: grant_type=password
Kc --> Oidc : 200 { access_token, refresh_token, expires_in, token_type }
Oidc --> Svc : TokenResponse
deactivate Oidc

== 6. the answer ==

Svc -> Svc : response.userUid(userUid)
Svc --> Ctl : 201 { accessToken, refreshToken,\n     expiresIn, tokenType, userUid }
deactivate Svc

@enduml


@startuml admin-rollback
title What happens when step 4 fails

skinparam shadowing false

start

:person-service created the domain user;
:Keycloak created the account
(no password yet);

if (PUT reset-password succeeded?) then (yes)
  :login and answer the client;
  stop
else (no)
  note right
    The account exists but can never
    be logged into, and the address is
    already taken: every later attempt
    to register it answers
    409 (Conflict) - forever.
  end note
  :DELETE /admin/realms/{realm}/users/{id};
  note right
    Compensating action. The address is
    free again, the caller can simply
    try once more.

    The domain user in person-service is
    NOT rolled back - its contract has no
    delete operation. Recorded as a known
    gap in CONTEXT.md.
  end note
  :answer the client with the original failure;
  stop
endif

@enduml


@startuml admin-me
title GET /v1/auth/me - why the Admin gateway is needed here too

skinparam shadowing false

rectangle "Access token (already verified)" as Tok {
  card "sub            -> keycloakUserId" as c1
  card "email          -> email" as c2
  card "given_name     -> firstName" as c3
  card "family_name    -> lastName" as c4
  card "email_verified -> emailVerified" as c5
  card "realm_access.roles -> roles" as c6
  card "user_uid       -> userUid" as c7
}

rectangle "Admin API\nGET /admin/realms/{realm}/users/{sub}" as Adm {
  card "createdTimestamp -> registeredAt" as c8
}

rectangle "CurrentUserResponse\n8 required fields" as Out

Tok --> Out : seven fields, no network call
Adm --> Out : the eighth one

note bottom of Tok
  user_uid is in the token because the realm
  declares a protocol mapper for it. Without
  that mapper this field would need a second
  admin call.
end note

note bottom of Adm
  registeredAt exists nowhere in the token,
  so /me costs one admin read. The alternative
  would be storing it on our side, and this
  service owns no database.
end note

@enduml


@startuml admin-classes
title Where the Admin gateway sits

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0

package service {
  class RegistrationService
  class AuthenticationService
}

package "gateway.keycloak" {
  class KeycloakAdminGateway <<@Component>> {
    + Mono<String> createUser(email, firstName,\n                         lastName, userUid)
    + Mono<Void> setPassword(id, password)
    + Mono<Void> deleteUser(id)
    + Mono<KeycloakUserResponse> findById(id)
  }
  class KeycloakOidcGateway <<@Component>> {
    + Mono<TokenResponse> login(email, password)
    + Mono<TokenResponse> refresh(token)
    + Mono<String> serviceAccountToken()
  }
  class KeycloakUserRequest <<record>> {
    username, email, firstName, lastName,
    enabled, emailVerified, attributes
  }
  class KeycloakUserResponse <<record>> {
    id, email, firstName, lastName,
    emailVerified, attributes,
    createdTimestamp
  }
  class KeycloakCredential <<record>> {
    type, value, temporary
  }
  class KeycloakErrorTranslator <<static>> {
    + Mono<Throwable> translate(response)
  }
}

package config {
  class KeycloakProperties <<record>> {
    tokenUri()
    usersUri()
  }
  class HttpClientsConfig {
    keycloakWebClient
    personsApi
  }
}

RegistrationService --> "com.dezxxx.person.client.api.PersonsApi" : step 1, user_uid
RegistrationService --> KeycloakAdminGateway
RegistrationService --> KeycloakOidcGateway
AuthenticationService --> KeycloakOidcGateway
AuthenticationService --> KeycloakAdminGateway : /me reads registeredAt

KeycloakAdminGateway --> KeycloakOidcGateway : needs a service token
KeycloakAdminGateway ..> KeycloakUserRequest : body of POST /users
KeycloakAdminGateway ..> KeycloakCredential : body of PUT /reset-password
KeycloakAdminGateway ..> KeycloakUserResponse : receives
KeycloakAdminGateway ..> KeycloakErrorTranslator : shared with the OIDC gateway
KeycloakAdminGateway --> KeycloakProperties : usersUri()
HttpClientsConfig --> KeycloakAdminGateway : keycloakWebClient

note right of KeycloakCredential
  Not part of KeycloakUserRequest any more.
  The handout sets the password through its
  own endpoint, so this record travels alone
  as the body of that call.
end note

note right of KeycloakErrorTranslator
  Already written. Both gateways translate
  Keycloak failures through the same table,
  so 409 means "email taken" no matter which
  call produced it.
end note

@enduml
